<section xml:id="sec-mod3-practice-set">
  <title>Module 3: Practice Problem Set</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 90 minutes</p>

    <p>
      Problems are labeled <m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> (Foundational),
      <m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> (Standard), and <m>[\mathbf{C}]</m>
      (Challenge). Work in order within each level; later problems
      in a level often build on earlier ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      These problems are not submitted, but you should write out
      complete solutions. For any problem that asks you to
      <em>prove</em> something, apply the standards from the
      Worked Examples: complete sentences, quantifiers stated
      explicitly, every step justified.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       FOUNDATIONAL
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod3-ps-f">
    <title>Foundational Problems</title>
    <!-- TODO(quinn): sentence added in #4 to satisfy the schema; confirm or replace. -->
    <p>
      These problems practice the basic moves of the module: computing
      intersections and set differences, deciding statements about
      membership and subsets, testing functions for injectivity and
      surjectivity, describing inverses of bijections, and classifying
      sets as finite, countably infinite, or uncountable.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-f1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Set Operations</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A = \{n \in \Z : n \text{ is even}\}</m>,
        <m>B = \{n \in \Z : n \text{ is a multiple of } 3\}</m>, and
        <m>C = \{n \in \Z : n \gt 0\}</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Is <m>6 \in A \cap B</m>? Is <m>9 \in A \cap B</m>?
            Justify each answer.
          </li>
          <li>
            Describe <m>A \cap B</m> in words. (Hint: what integers
            are both even and divisible by 3?)
          </li>
          <li>
            Describe <m>A \cap B \cap C</m> in words.
          </li>
          <li>
            Give an element of <m>B \setminus A</m>. Give an element
            of <m>A \setminus B</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>6 \in A \cap B</m> (since 6 is even and divisible by 3);
        <m>9 \notin A \cap B</m> (since 9 is not even).
        (b) <m>A \cap B</m> is the set of multiples of 6.
        (c) <m>A \cap B \cap C</m> is the set of positive multiples of 6:
        <m>\{6, 12, 18, \ldots\}</m>.
        (d) <m>3 \in B \setminus A</m>; <m>2 \in A \setminus B</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-f2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Membership and Subsets</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each of the following, decide whether the statement is true
        or false. If true, give a brief justification; if false, give
        a counterexample.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\emptyset \subseteq \{1, 2, 3\}</m></li>
          <li><m>\{1\} \in \{\{1\}, \{2\}, \{3\}\}</m></li>
          <li><m>1 \in \{\{1\}, \{2\}, \{3\}\}</m></li>
          <li>If <m>A \subseteq B</m> and <m>B \subseteq A</m>, then <m>A = B</m>.</li>
          <li>
            <m>\{n \in \N : n^2 \lt 10\} = \{1, 2, 3\}</m>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) True: the empty set is a subset of every set.
        (b) True: <m>\{1\}</m> is one of the three elements of this set.
        (c) False: the elements of <m>\{\{1\},\{2\},\{3\}\}</m> are
        sets, not numbers; <m>1</m> is not one of them.
        (d) True: this is the Axiom of Extensionality.
        (e) True: <m>1^2=1 \lt 10</m>, <m>2^2=4 \lt 10</m>,
        <m>3^2=9 \lt 10</m>, <m>4^2=16 \geq 10</m>, so the set is
        exactly <m>\{1,2,3\}</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-f3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Identifying Function Properties</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each function, determine (with a brief justification but
        without a formal proof) whether it is injective, surjective,
        bijective, or none of these. If it is bijective, describe
        the inverse function.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <m>f : \R \to \R</m>, <m>f(x) = x^3</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>g : \R \to [0,\infty)</m>, <m>g(x) = x^2</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>h : \N \to \N</m>, <m>h(n) = n + 1</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>k : \Z \to \Z</m>, <m>k(n) = n - 7</m>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Bijective. Every real number has a unique real cube root;
        <m>f^{-1}(y) = y^{1/3}</m>.
        (b) Not injective (since <m>g(-1)=g(1)=1</m>), but surjective
        (every nonneg. real is the square of its square root).
        (c) Injective but not surjective: <m>1 \in \N</m> has no
        preimage (there is no <m>n \in \N</m> with <m>n+1=1</m>,
        since <m>n=0 \notin \N</m> by the convention <m>\N = \{1,2,\ldots\}</m>).
        (d) Bijective; <m>k^{-1}(m) = m + 7</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-f4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Countable or Uncountable?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each set below, state (without proof) whether it is finite,
        countably infinite, or uncountable. Briefly explain your
        reasoning.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>The set of prime numbers.</li>
          <li>The set of irrational numbers.</li>
          <li>The set <m>\{1/n : n \in \N\}</m>.</li>
          <li>The interval <m>(0,1)</m>.</li>
          <li>The set <m>\Q \times \Q</m>.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Countably infinite (a subset of <m>\N</m>; infinite by
        Euclid's theorem).
        (b) Uncountable (if it were countable, then
        <m>\R = \Q \cup (\R \setminus \Q)</m> would be a countable
        union of countable sets, contradicting Theorem 1.21).
        (c) Countably infinite (in bijection with <m>\N</m> via
        <m>n \mapsto 1/n</m>).
        (d) Uncountable (bijection with <m>\R</m> via, e.g.,
        <m>x \mapsto \tan(\pi x - \pi/2)</m>; or directly from
        Theorem 1.21).
        (e) Countably infinite (product of two countable sets by
        Prop. 1.18).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       STANDARD
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod3-ps-s">
    <title>Standard Problems</title>
    <!-- TODO(quinn): sentence added in #4 to satisfy the schema; confirm or replace. -->
    <p>
      These problems apply the same moves to longer arguments: an
      element-chasing proof of a set equality, proofs that a function is
      or is not injective or surjective, finding a range and an inverse,
      and proving a set countably infinite with an explicit bijection.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-s1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Element-Chasing: De Morgan's Law</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> be subsets of a universal
        set <m>U</m>, with complements taken relative to <m>U</m>.
        Prove:
        <md>C \setminus (A \cup B) = (C \setminus A) \cap (C \setminus B).</md>
        Your proof must use the element-chasing method: show each set
        is a subset of the other.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Recall that <m>x \in C \setminus A</m> means <m>x \in C</m>
        and <m>x \notin A</m>. Use the fact that
        <m>x \notin A \cup B</m> is equivalent to
        <m>x \notin A</m> and <m>x \notin B</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-s2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Proving Injectivity and Non-surjectivity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f : \Z \to \Z</m> be defined by <m>f(n) = 3n - 1</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>Prove that <m>f</m> is injective.</li>
          <li>Prove that <m>f</m> is not surjective.</li>
          <li>
            Identify the range of <m>f</m> and describe it as a
            familiar subset of <m>\Z</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-s3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Proving Bijectivity and Finding the Inverse</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define <m>f : \N \to \N</m> by
        <md>f(n) = \begin{cases} n + 1 \amp \text{if } n \text{ is odd,} \\ n - 1 \amp \text{if } n \text{ is even.}\end{cases}</md>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Verify <m>f</m> on the first six values:
            <m>f(1), f(2), \ldots, f(6)</m>.
          </li>
          <li>Prove that <m>f</m> is injective.</li>
          <li>Prove that <m>f</m> is surjective.</li>
          <li>
            Find <m>f^{-1}</m>. (You may be surprised by the answer.)
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For part (b): if <m>f(m) = f(n)</m>, consider the cases where
        <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are both odd, both even, or have different
        parity.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-s4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Even Integers Are Countably Infinite</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>E = \{2n : n \in \Z\}</m> be the set of all even integers.
        Prove that <m>E</m> is countably infinite by constructing an
        explicit bijection <m>g : \N \to E</m>. Verify that your function
        is both injective and surjective.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Use the bijection <m>\N \to \Z</m> from Worked Example 4, then
        compose with <m>n \mapsto 2n</m>. Alternatively, define a direct
        bijection by interleaving positive and negative even integers.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       CHALLENGE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod3-ps-c">
    <title>Challenge Problems</title>
    <!-- TODO(quinn): sentence added in #4 to satisfy the schema; confirm or replace. -->
    <p>
      These problems extend the module's ideas: partial converses, with a
      counterexample, to the composition results for injections and
      surjections, and a proof by Cantor's diagonal argument that
      <m>(0,1)</m> is uncountable.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-c1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> A Partial Converse for Composition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f : A \to B</m> and <m>g : B \to C</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Prove: if <m>g \circ f</m> is injective, then <m>f</m>
            is injective.
          </li>
          <li>
            Give a specific example showing that <m>g</m> need not be
            injective: find sets <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m> and
            functions <m>f</m>, <m>g</m> such that <m>g \circ f</m>
            is injective but <m>g</m> is not.
          </li>
          <li>
            State and prove the analogous partial converse for
            surjectivity: if <m>g \circ f</m> is surjective, which
            of <m>f</m> or <m>g</m> must be surjective?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For (a): suppose <m>f(a_1) = f(a_2)</m> and apply <m>g</m> to
        both sides; use injectivity of <m>g \circ f</m>.
        For (c): the answer is <m>g</m>; to prove it, let <m>c \in C</m>
        and use surjectivity of <m>g \circ f</m> to find <m>a \in A</m>
        with <m>(g \circ f)(a) = c</m>, then conclude about <m>g</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-ps-c2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Cantor's Diagonal Argument &#x2014; Guided Proof</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In this problem you will write a complete proof that the
        interval <m>(0,1)</m> is uncountable, following Cantor's
        diagonal argument. Fill in each step with a complete
        mathematical justification.
      </p>
      <p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
          <p>
            State the assumption for contradiction: what are you
            assuming about <m>(0,1)</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Under this assumption, how can you represent the elements
            of <m>(0,1)</m>? (Describe the <q>list</q> and the grid
            of decimal digits.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Describe the construction of the diagonal number <m>x</m>.
            Be specific: for each position <m>i</m>, how do you choose
            the <m>i</m>th digit of <m>x</m>? Why do you avoid using
            0 or 9? (Hint: think about <m>0.999\ldots</m>.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>x \in (0,1)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>x</m> is not equal to any element in the list.
            For a specific <m>r_n</m>, at which decimal digit does <m>x</m>
            differ from <m>r_n</m>? Why does this guarantee
            <m>x \neq r_n</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            State the contradiction and conclude.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</section>
